---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../data/site-config';
import { VALUE } from '../../content/home';
import { KIT as K } from '../../lib/kit';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Sonographer By State 2026: Who Publishes This License Guide';
const description = 'Sonographer By State in 2026: who publishes this guide to the license rules for sonographers and x-ray technologists, its sources, and what it never sells.';
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif">
    <h1>About Sonographer By State</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why this license guide exists</h2>
    <p>Someone choosing between ultrasound and x-ray, or moving to another state, asks plain questions: does my state require a license, which agency issues it, what do the national exams cost, and which schools near me train for it. The answers are spread over {K.C.total} agency sites and two credentialing bodies, and many pages that rank first collect contact details for schools or repeat claims without a source.</p>
    <p>{VALUE}</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The site is published by {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds calculators and guides on public rules. It is an independent site, not a government site, not a licensing board and not affiliated with ARRT (American Registry of Radiologic Technologists, the national x-ray certification board), ARDMS (American Registry for Diagnostic Medical Sonography, the national ultrasound certification board), Inteleos (which runs the ARDMS exams) or the ASRT (American Society of Radiologic Technologists). It sells nothing, enrolls no one, takes no commission, carries no affiliate link and asks for no contact details.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>What it relies on</h2>
    <p>The state table of the American Society of Radiologic Technologists, the pages of the state agencies it points to ({K.C.read} of {K.C.total} read, the others could not be opened by our software), the ARRT and Inteleos fee pages, and the IPEDS files of the National Center for Education Statistics. All were read on {K.day(K.READ)}.</p>
    <h2>What it leaves out</h2>
    <p>Medical content of any kind, salaries, and whether a person qualifies for a license. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a> lists every limit and the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> explains corrections.</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, for a rule that changed or a figure we read wrong. We answer questions about a rule, not about your own application, which only your state agency can decide.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
